Description: screenShield: reuse the unlock dialog instead of recreating it
 Every lock/unlock cycle allocates a brand new UnlockDialog (password entry,
 clock, notifications box, background actors, GPU-backed Cogl textures) and
 destroys the old one. GJS/SpiderMonkey does not run garbage collection
 between cycles, so the destroyed Clutter actor tree's memory accumulates as
 anonymous heap (Private_Dirty) rather than being reclaimed promptly -
 roughly 4-10 MB per lock/unlock cycle, growing linearly with usage.
 .
 This keeps a single UnlockDialog instance alive for the life of the
 ScreenShield and just hides/re-shows it instead of destroy()/recreate.
 The dialog's inner AuthPrompt (the actual verification state) is already
 lazily created and destroyed by _ensureAuthPrompt()/_maybeDestroyAuthPrompt()
 in unlockDialog.js on every clock<->prompt page switch, so no stale
 authentication state is retained across cycles - only the static shell
 chrome (background, clock, layout) is now reused.
 .
 _ensureUnlockDialog() is restructured so dialog construction (and the
 'failed'/'wake-up-screen' signal connections) happens only once, while
 _refreshBackground() and dialog.open() (which shows the actor and
 acquires/re-acquires the modal grab) run on every call, since those need
 to happen each time the dialog is (re)shown.
 .
 A new resetToClock() method is added to UnlockDialog so that
 _completeDeactivate() can snap the dialog back to the clock page (and
 destroy the AuthPrompt) before hiding, preventing stale prompt state
 from carrying over to the next lock cycle.
